        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> > @echasnovski Here's a minimal configuration you can reproduce with:\r\n> > [nvim.zip](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/files/12641407/nvim.zip)\r\n> \r\n> @echasnovski In brief, the problem can be summarized as follows: mini.starter does not provide padding below, so if window size exceeds the information displayed by starter, endofbuffer takes over - with most colorschemes, this isn't a problem as they typically ensure that eob shares both foreground and background colors with \"Normal\" - but there are still some instances where this may not hold true\r\n\r\nYes, this is how 'EndOfBuffer' highlights works. And yes, I can reproduce this with your setup.\r\n\r\nThe problem here is that [`EndOfBuffer` has no background](https://github.com/sainnhe/everforest/blob/83b666410d7ae0eccf96dbbe3b4b6ac5b8172d38/colors/everforest.vim#L59) for any combination of settings. While in your setup it *has* explicit background. The output of `:hi EndOfBuffer` is `EndOfBuffer    xxx ctermfg=239 ctermbg=233 guifg=#495156 guibg=#1e2326`.\r\n\r\nWithout 'folke/lazy.nvim' using built-in package manager I can not reproduce this. Here are the steps:\r\n- Put whole [sainnhe/everforest](https://github.com/sainnhe/everforest) and [mini.starter](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.starter) in 'pack/plugins/opt' directory.\r\n- Create the following 'init.lua':\r\n```lua\r\nvim.o.termguicolors = true\r\n\r\nvim.g.everforest_background = [[hard]]\r\nvim.g.everforest_dim_inactive_windows = 1\r\nvim.cmd('packadd everforest')\r\nvim.cmd('color everforest')\r\n\r\nvim.cmd('packadd mini.starter')\r\nrequire('mini.starter').setup()\r\n```\r\n- Open neovim with this config. I tested with `NVIM_APPNAME`.\r\n\r\nI am not really sure what is going on here. Maybe 'folke/lazy.nvim' somehow affects the highlight groups, but quick searching its code base didn't reveal anything.",
